# Acute Limb Ischaemia

- **Etiology:**
  - Embolic in nature - not on a background of intermittent claudication with a source of emboli (e.g. AF)
  - Thrombosis in situ - on a background of chronic arterial insufficiency
  - Trauma (rare)
- **Pathophysiology of acute limb ischaemia:**
  - Stasis proximal to occlusion - thrombus extension distal to proximal
  - Limb ischaemia - results in the P's of limb ischaemia
- **Clinical features of acute limb ischaemia** - 6 P's of acute limb ischaemia:
  - Parasthesia - progressive numbess
  - Paralysis - typically inability to move toes distally, but is ascending and progressive in nature
  - Pain - ischaemic pain of skin and subcutaneous tissue as a result of supply-driven ischaemia
  - Pallor - due to loss of blood flow
  - Pulselessness - dependent on site of lesion, where distal pulses are lost, while proximal pulses become thrusting
  - Perishing cold - non-specific sign, dependent on external temperature by which the limb equilibrates with
- **Emergency evaluation and Mx:**
  - Dx - purely clinical (based on -ve claudication Hx, source of emboli, and pulselessness)
  - Immediate anticoagulation - IV heparin 5000U (to reduce extension of emboli)
  - Immediate surgical intervention or thrombolysis

## Amputation

- **Indications of amputation in peripheral arterial diseases:**
  - <u>Dead limb</u> (actual dead tissue) - infarction of macroscopic portion of tissue, resulting in dry gangrene (may be from PAD, or small vessel diseases)
  - <u>Deadly limb</u> (threaten viablle tissue) - putrefaction and superimposed infection of gangrenous area with capacity to spread to surrounding viable tissue, i.e. wet gangrene
  - <u>Dead lost limb</u> (severe effects on QoL, but relief impossible) - severe rest pain even in absence of tissue loss, reconstruction is deemed impossible

  
  ![](img/amputation_indications.png)
- **Distal and transmatatarsal amputation** - revascularisation required prior to amputation
- **Major amputation** - below-knee amputation (BKA) vs aboe-knee amputation:
  - **Considerations for choice of operation:**
    - **Site of arterial disease** - absent femoral pulse indicates above-knee amputation due to poor healing (but presence of femoral pulse does not guarantee healing of BKA and may subsequently require revision)
    - **Prospect of walking** - BKA provides best chance of walking with a prosthesis
    - **Healing** - Above-knee operations are more likely to heal than BKA
- **Post-operative care of amputee:**
  - Pain relief - opiates given regularly
  - Pressure ulcer Mx of contralateral limb - may delay mobilisation
  - Regular exercise - prevent flexion contractures, build up muscle power and coordination
  - Monitor for complications
- **Complications of amputation:**
  - Early complications - haeorrhage, haematoma, infection +/- abscess formation, wound dehiscnence and gangrene off flaps, DVT and PE (requires subcutaneous heparin prophylaxis)
  - Late complications - pain (unresolved infection, bone spur, scar adherent to bone, amputation neuroma), phantom limb and pain, ulceration of stump due to pressure effects of prosthesis
